SM(455,{"n":"Acts 6","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Acts 6.mp3","d":3058.6,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.8,"Okay, so when we get started in just a minute, we're gonna be picking up in uh Acts of the Apostles, uh, chapter six, verse one."],[28.24,"Uh as I always say, uh, by the time someone hears this online, uh you'll be able to go and hear the first uh Bible study of the book of Acts online and hear my long introduction to the book uh to get us caught up to where we are now in chapter six."],[45.2,"Um we've really been still focusing on sort of the first phase, as we said, uh, at the beginning in St."],[54.0,"Luke, when he gives his account of the ascension, his second account of the ascension after the end of the Gospel of Luke."],[59.44,"Here, remember uh Christ told the apostles they'd be as witnesses in Jerusalem and then all of Judea and then Samaria and then to the ends of the earth."],[67.32,"We're still in the Jerusalem phase of that."],[70.36,"Uh we're still in the city and its sort of immediate surroundings."],[74.44,"Uh we've seen the coming of the Holy Spirit on Pentecost, which sort of began the growth uh of uh the church of Christianity, uh, from the 120 people sitting in the room that we started with, uh, to now thousands uh coming in and not only coming in, but also going out as they, you know, they were there for the Feast of Pentecost, they've been there for the other feasts, they're hearing and they're going back home and taking uh what they've heard with them."],[104.6,"with them. Uh and so we'll meet up some more some of again later on"],[110.0,"Um we've also seen some people, uh, like we saw uh Barnabas already, at least we got a glimpse of him, who had who was from Cyprus uh originally, who had sold his property there and given the money."],[123.76,"Uh the church community is now living in Jerusalem as part of the Christian community."],[128.0,"Uh, we've seen their way of life."],[129.92,"Uh we've seen them uh continuing to preach in the temple, which remembers what Christ was doing uh shortly before the crucifixion for a week."],[139.12,"And similarly to the way in which the Jewish leaders uh came to crucify him, they have also been now opposing uh the apostles, his disciples as, they've continued that preaching and continued the healings and the working of miracles there in and around sort of the temple complex."],[157.72,"We saw them get arrested now twice."],[161.0,"That's uh the first time they were given a stern warning."],[163.88,"The second time we saw sort of all of the rabbinical big guns brought in, including Gamaliel, who we saw last time, um, to uh issue them more dire warnings."],[175.72,"Uh and uh Gamaliel sort of counseled caution."],[179.0,"He reminded them, remember, of some of the would-be messiahs who had shown up recently, and how, you know, when it was shown that they weren't really Messiahs when they had been killed, sort of their movements petered out, you know, and so he said, Well, you know, we're we we could tell we're still pretty close to Christ's actual crucifixion because he's saying, Well, look, you know, if if if this isn't from God, if he isn't really the Messiah, then this is just sort of the death roes of the movement, you know, and it's gonna peter out now."],[209.6,"Um, and if it doesn't, that'll be pretty good evidence that it's from God, and so then maybe we shouldn't do anything."],[216.88,"You know. Um, so he based on that, he said, whether it's true or not, the best thing we can do right now is nothing."],[224.48,"So and uh they sort of took that advice."],[227.36,"They did remember uh have the dis the apostles beaten before they let them go."],[232.96,"Uh, but other than that, uh they did nothing."],[235.6,"We saw the apostles were not only rejoiced at the fact that they had suffered for their faith, but sort of went out even more vigorously and energized to go and preach in the city of Jerusalem."],[248.04,"So that's really where we're picking up now here at the beginning of chapter six."],[252.84,"So unless there are any questions or comments or anything else left over from last time, we'll go ahead and get started in chapter six, verse one."],[263.32,"Now, in those days when the number of the disciples was multiplying, there arose a complaint against the Hebrews by the Hellenists because their widows were neglected in the daily distribution."],[274.2,"Okay So the code there at the beginning in those days with the disciples were multiplying means as we've already seen, things are growing, we've got more and more people coming into this community."],[285.2,"We've seen that for the most part the people in this community have been sort of selling what they have and then all living together and sharing their goods, sharing their food, sharing their well-being together."],[297.6,"Well, as a community like that gets bigger, you start running into logistical logistical problems."],[304.85,"So um one of these logistical problems has to do with the care of the widows who are part of that community."],[313.49,"Widows and orphans, sort of throughout the scriptures, because throughout the ancient world, they are sort of the endangered class of people, because um only adult men could testify in court, only adult men could go out and get employ what we would consider employment."],[329.25,"No one was going to hire a woman to be a farmhand"],[342.41,"So widows and orphans kind of had no one to protect them."],[346.49,"And so God, very early in the in the Torah in the Pentateuch, says that He is the protector of widows and orphans."],[354.25,"He is the one who will stand up for their cause."],[356.97,"And he will oppose anyone who takes advantage of or harms them."],[362.17,"And so they're a very important part in this community."],[365.45,"It's very important to them that when the food is distributed every day."],[370.03,"Because rather than people holding on to their own money and going out and buying food, they're sort of throwing it. into this pot, and then food is gotten and distributed"],[378.23,"It's very important to make sure that the widows, the orphans, the other people are getting, you know, what they need every day."],[385.59,"And so we have these two groups here who start to come into conflict."],[390.79,"And they're referred to here as the Hebrews of the Hellenists."],[394.68,"And uh Hellenist, uh, strictly speaking, uh Hellene is a Greek."],[401.14,"But these are not Jews and Greeks, these are two different types of Jews."],[406.26,"And uh what the two terms are being used to describe is the language they speak."],[413.8,"There are there are Jews who are going to be Jews who liviveed who are nat to this area around Jerusalem who speak who speak Hebrew."],[424.48,"Aramaic, I'm sure."],[426.16,"No, not here."],[429.76,"The linguistic situation in Palestine was very complicated at this point in history."],[437.76,"But for the most part, anyone who had any level of education, or anyone who was from, say Galilee."],[447.74,"Wh whichich Galilee is called Galilee the Gentiles because there were a lot of Greek speaking cities, a lot of Roman cities there."],[455.94,"Um so anyone there who's doing commerce, like the disciples who were fishermen who are going to be selling their wares in a market or anything. everybody had to learn Greek, you know, to do to do business"],[465.86,"They would know anyone educated and anyone in that kind of situation where they were working with Gentiles would know Greek."],[471.78,"Um the common people, your peasants for most of Palestine, their native language would be Aramaic."],[480.42,"Um in the in Jerusalem itself and the area around it, there was a type of Hebrew."],[487.7,"Um you have to remember that Hebrew is basically a dialect of Aramaic."],[492.58,"Uh-huh. They're very similar languages."],[495.86,"Um but um there was a a dialect of Hebrew that was spoken sort of just there."],[503.78,"That's not identical to like the Hebrew of the Old Testament."],[508.14,"Which only the very educated, you know, rabbis, you know, the Pharisees, the scribes, they would know that type of Hebrew."],[516.62,"But there was a little bit of a different dialect."],[521.82,"So what's really, even though on the surface they're using the term Hebrew and Hellenist, you know, Greek speaking, Hebrew speaking, this is really the native population of Jerusalem."],[534.4,"Who are so who are part of this community."],[536.97,"Who have lived there the whole time."],[538.81,"And then other Jews from the diaspora, from the rest of the world who are primarily Greek speaking."],[547.21,"But that would include even Galileans and that kind of thing, who have now. come and joined the Christian community and so are living in Jerusalem"],[557.33,"Like Barnabas from Barnabas would be a perfect example of one of them, because he was from Cyprus."],[563.49,"And so he would have been primarily Greek speaking, you know, uh living on Cyprus."],[569.49,"So that that's really the two groups here."],[572.29,"The core here of the two groups is there are people who are natives of Jerusalem and its surroundings who are part of the community, and then there are other people who have come from outside and joined this community."],[582.53,"And the people who have come from outside, who sort of aren't the locals, come with this complaint that the widows in their group, the widows from other places, aren't being paid. as much attention to as sort of the locals who everybody knows"],[603.77,"Would have the Hellenist widows made the pilgrimage?"],[607.37,"Or or not?"],[609.05,"Well, people are going, they're distributing food every day."],[613.51,"And they're saying, look, you know, our widows aren't being treated the same way, aren't being paid as much attention to as sort of the local members of the community who everybody knows."],[625.59,"You do raise a question though that most of these people who are not from Jerusalem came originally on pilgrimage."],[636.59,"Uh and this I would have pictured would be expensive."],[641.95,"The widows wouldn't be it could be, or unless they were sponsored by relatives."],[647.77,"You could have just walked, walked and stayed with a relative, you know, traveled on foot from wherever."],[652.91,"You know, and then stayed with a relative."],[655.47,"Um they camp in different stations to pay each to bed to Jerusalem."],[661.55,"So it's a long journey."],[662.67,"It's not like uh if they travel for three days or one week."],[666.81,"It's a long journey."],[668.54,"And so that was that was not impossible."],[671.18,"Um but so this is this is an insiders outsiders thing."],[676.7,"You know, this is we've got these newcomers, these new sort of converts, and then we've got sort of the locals."],[682.44,"The local community."],[683.48,"And the local community is doing a very good job of sort of taking care of each other because they all know each other and everything, but they're not necessarily paying as much attention to these new people who have come in."],[699.36,"Then the 12 summoned the multitude of the disciples and said, okay, so they gather."],[704.86,"The twelve, gravel gather everybody."],[708.19,"And there's everybody called disciples now."],[711.68,"And said, and and it's important, whose disciples are they?"],[715.92,"Well, immediately. They're disciples of the twelve of the apostles."],[721.25,"These are people who have come and are learning from."],[723.59,"They're learning from that's what disciple means, the word for disciple in Greek means learner."],[726.55,"So they're they're they're there learning from the apostles"],[731.85,"But of course, ultimately they're disciples of Christ through the apostles, because they're teaching."],[737.85,"As uh St."],[738.57,"Luke said up at the beginning of Acts, they're continuing to teach what Christ taught."],[744.49,"Um but so they gather gather everyone together, and they say, It is not desirable that we should leave the Word of God and serve tables."],[756.73,"So they say, okay, look."],[759.74,"We have, you know, we're we're preaching."],[763.36,"Now, note here the Word of God, and this is important."],[766.96,"I've probably said this before, but it bears repeating."],[769.68,"We're very conditioned in modern American society when we hear word of God to think Bible"],[777.4,"There is no Bible at this point in time."],[783.08,"So they're not talking about the Bible."],[785.56,"When they talk about the Word of God, they're talking about, in this case, preaching."],[791.3,"In this case, preaching."],[793.46,"Because they, like the prophets who had the Holy Spirit, it's the Word of God, that they're proclaiming, not their own opinion or their own teaching."],[804.12,"So that's that's what they're talking about."],[806.28,"They're not saying, well, we need to be about Bible study."],[809.56,"And you know, you guys go and do this."],[812.52,"Um, even the Old Testament, which of course exists, all those books exist, they're not in a book."],[821.21,"Remember I they're in individual scrolls."],[824.31,"Individual um, and uh everybody didn't own them."],[830.23,"Um synagogues would have to have vast amounts of money donated to them just to buy a Torah scroll."],[836.31,"So say Peter didn't have one that he carried around with him so he could study it at night."],[841.13,"That's so that's not that's not what's in view here."],[844.49,"But they say, you know, we we this is what we're doing, and this is what they've been tasked to do."],[849.21,"This is what Jesus told them."],[850.25,"He said, You're gonna be my witness, you're gonna go out and bear witness."],[853.15,"So they're saying, look, we're we're out doing this."],[855.83,"We acknowledge that there are these problems going on."],[858.89,"We hear you, we hear your complaints, but we can't stop preaching in order to manage the finer points of the food distribution."],[868.89,"Because you know, the the food distribution is important, but we have our own."],[874.19,"We have our own tasks."],[875.55,"And this is this is parallel."],[877.63,"We've been talking as we've been going through the book of Acts about a lot of these parallels to that St."],[882.35,"Luke is bringing out to the to the Old Testament."],[884.91,"Remember, and we mentioned this before, but when we're talking about the Sanhedrin, that uh there's that episode where Jethro comes and visits Moses, and he sees that because Moses, you know, is the leader, he's sitting there all day, and people are coming to him with all these disputes."],[903.19,"You know, so and so killed my goat, you know, so and so did this, so and so did that, you know, resolve all these little disputes."],[910.39,"And Jethro's his father in law says, Look, you know, you can't you can't do this all day, every day."],[917.59,"You need to appoint some people, and that's when the 70 elders are appointed over the tribes to go and resolve these things and and handle these things."],[926.63,"And so there's a similar thing going on here."],[929.76,"It's not that the resolution of these things isn't important, it's just that there's a particular calling here on the apostles that they need to fulfill."],[939.36,"And so, as we're gonna see, the solution isn't so quick complaining."],[945.0,"The solution is we're going to now bring in, just as Moses brought in the elders, we're going to now bring in some people to oversee this because it's important and to make sure that this happens."],[955.08,"This happens in the correct way."],[961.56,"Therefore, brethren, seek out from among you seven men of good reputation, full of the Holy Spirit and wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business, but we will give ourselves continually to prayer and to the ministry of the word."],[975.48,"So they say, okay, we're gonna this is what we're called to, this is what we're gonna do."],[980.22,"We're gonna spend our time in prayer and in preaching."],[983.52,"The word of God, preaching about Christ."],[986.72,"You pick seven people."],[990.02,"And the seven comes not only from the seven D, you know, before, but also uh seven is a number throughout the scriptures that represents completion or wholeness."],[1001.94,"Um that's related to the seven days of creation idea idea that the whole you have the whole world like Zembi, the whole thing."],[1010.58,"But even that really derives from, and I know this is gonna sound a little crazy in our modern world, but because there are seven planets, which is how they understood things in the ancient world."],[1025.78,"Because again, we don't have telescopes, we don't have observatories."],[1029.2,"And so you've got stars that move in sort of these fixed patterns in the different seasons."],[1033.73,"They could see that."],[1034.85,"And then the word planet in Greek comes from the word that means wanderer."],[1039.25,"They notice that there are these seven objects that they could see in the sky at night that didn't sort of follow the same patterns of the stars."],[1048.29,"They seemed to sort of wander around."],[1051.01,"And of course, that's because they didn't understand the solar system and all this."],[1054.61,"That's why they called them wanderers."],[1057.09,"And the sun was one of them."],[1058.77,"The sun and the moon were two of them."],[1061.41,"So but so anything that wasn't in a constellation."],[1066.89,"That moved independently of."],[1068.63,"And so because there were seven of those, all of those meant sort of the whole cosmos."],[1075.88,"And their reasoning was that the brighter and the bigger it was, the closer it was"],[1084.08,"So it went for them sun, moon, Venus, what we now know to be Venus."],[1090.85,"You could understand from their perspective."],[1092.77,"But so because of that, the number seven game this that this is the whole complete thing."],[1098.53,"You know, when you have seven of something."],[1103.01,"So yeah, so we have these we're gonna we're gonna appoint these seven seven men, and they're to be of good reputation, full of the Holy Spirit and wisdom."],[1112.05,"So this isn't the new guy who just showed up right last week."],[1118.83,"These were people people, uh, and traditionally, these are who came from the 70."],[1119.79,"Remember, Jesus had the 70 disciples"],[1125.35,"There were sort of the Peter, James, and John, and then the 12, and then the 70."],[1129.91,"And then uh traditionally, these people all came from the 70s."],[1132.63,"So these are people who had not just been around for what we've seen before in the book of Acts, but these are people who were around with Jesus, meaning, you know, they've they've been with us a long time, they they know what's going on."],[1143.99,"They're not recent quote unquote converts."],[1147.75,"They're not, you know, newcomers, and they're not super young people who you know aren't steady and stable."],[1156.63,"Um the saying please the whole multitude, so everybody pretty much agrees."],[1163.99,"Okay, this is a good idea."],[1166.75,"And they chose Stephen, a man full of faith in the Holy Spirit, and Philip, Prochorus, Niconor, Timon, Parmenus, and Nicholas, a proselyte from Antioch, whom they set before the apostles, and when they had prayed, they laid hands on them."],[1181.39,"Okay, so they choose these seven."],[1184.75,"Now, what says that Nicholas is a uh proselyte from Antioch, they don't mean he's a convert to Christianity from Antioch."],[1194.51,"Proselyte means he's not ethnically Jewish."],[1198.03,"Okay He's someone who was not Jewish, who entered the Jewish community at Antioch."],[1203.79,"That's why he has a Greek name."],[1206.35,"Nicolaus. He has a Greek name and he he uh had joined the Jewish community."],[1209.87,"That's what makes him a proselyte"],[1216.37,"And then now he has accepted Jesus as the Messiah."],[1220.85,"Okay. So the the the core there, the reason I mention he's a proselyte is that he's not Jewish ethnically."],[1228.59,"Well, we we don't have the word deacon yet."],[1233.71,"Not yet. Okay."],[1236.11,"Well I want to talk about that when we get to it, so late."],[1241.63,"Okay. Well, we can talk about it now."],[1244.27,"If you want to."],[1245.23,"Well, um, I was raised Presbyterian, and they have um elders and deacons."],[1253.31,"Yeah Presbyters and deacons."],[1254.45,"Yeah. Presbyters translated elder."],[1256.37,"Yeah. When when my ophthalmologist told me I had presbyopia, I laughed."],[1261.81,"That just means old eyes."],[1263.33,"Yeah. Actually, if you want to get really technical, the word presbyter originally, if you go way back in Greek, it means a person with a beard."],[1273.41,"Uh-huh. Oh, okay."],[1275.25,"So technically it's bearded eyes."],[1277.17,"If you no wonder I can't see that hair of my eyes."],[1281.97,"Um but uh so the way the way they think of a deacon is very much this way."],[1290.45,"The deacons are in charge of the practical matters."],[1293.82,"The presbyters are in charge of the of the church, of the spiritual life."],[1296.78,"of the And then yeah, the material, the deacons take care the treasury and money."],[1300.5,"Oh I meant."],[1304.82,"Yeah, the deacons do basically what our parish council does, and the uh pres the elders do other stuff."],[1314.74,"Yeah. Officiate. Yeah."],[1318.1,"So our deacons, on the other hand, the Orthodox Church, uh, do liturgical stuff."],[1324.47,"So what's what's the word really mean and what uh how did it come to mean yeah what it means for us?"],[1335.11,"Well the word the word presbyter um where was I going with that?"],[1340.87,"Oh the word the word the word presbyter, you know, as you mentioned just means older person."],[1342.15,"You know, um older man specifically"],[1345.07,"Uh-huh. Um and uh the same way, you know, it it doesn't tell you a lot about what presbyter is gonna mean in the church, because it's not just all the older men."],[1357.95,"Uh the same way uh the word diaconos uh just means servant, a household servant."],[1365.55,"Uh-huh. So it doesn't help you a lot with the yeah."],[1372.35,"Um so uh but um the the core of your question, I think, is is in terms of the functions of the deacon."],[1381.55,"And uh what we're gonna see here as this unfolds with the story of Stephen and with Philip and some of the other deacons is that these deacons are going out and preaching and evangelizing um and leading prayers and they're sort of doing all of these extra things beyond just right."],[1404.39,"If you just read this couple of paragraphs isolated, you say, Oh well they're just there for the food distribution."],[1410.4,"But they have as we're gonna see the book of Acts as it unfolds, they have these additional duties and roles as well."],[1417.52,"Uh and so what you see in the early church, uh if you look at the D Dock A, what it says about deacons from the beginning of the second century, um if you look at uh the Apostolic Constitutions and some of the other things from the the third and fourth century that talk about what deacons do."],[1435.0,"And we'll talk more about this also when we get into St."],[1439.08,"Paul's pastoral epistles, first and second Timothy and Titus, because there he talks about presbyters and deacons and what they do and the qualifications and all that."],[1447.48,"And we'll get into an interesting discussion about whether there were women deacons and what they did at that point."],[1454.52,"Here, though, these are all men."],[1462.68,"Well, we're going to see some deaconesses before we get out of Acts, too, so we may have part of that discussion then."],[1468.88,"But what we see in all of those sources is that they had this dual role."],[1476.24,"They had this liturgical role and preaching and teaching role on the one hand within the church, subsidiary to the bishop."],[1485.68,"But then they were also primarily charged with the visiting of the sick and bringing the Eucharist to them when they couldn't come to church and the elderly and widows and this kind of thing."],[1500.32,"So they had this sort of, it wasn't taking care of the finances of the church per se."],[1507.65,"Because churches, especially at this point in the book of Acts, aren't small nonprofit corporations the way some of them are today, or large nonprofit corporations, as some of them are, some of them are today."],[1518.69,"The U.S. So the that element was more focused on a ministry of mercy to the poor and to the sick."],[1528.41,"Seeing that food gets to they sort of served as an extension of the bishop's ministry was seen to take place primarily within the church, and then the deacons extended that ministry outside the physical boundaries of the church."],[1548.49,"So that like the apostles, the bishop could devote himself to prayer and to the preaching of the word and to teaching."],[1554.09,"And then the deacons would go out."],[1556.65,"And and for for centuries in the church, based on this passage, there were only seven deacons per city so the the then bishop of Antioch later patriarch of Antioch would have seven deacons right who are doing this ministry of mercy underneath him and in Jerusalem and in any other city um but so that was that was their primary task is that they they were extending but that included preaching that preaching and teaching ministry extending that outside the sort of the walls of of the church outside of the boundaries of the church um so that's that's really what we're gonna see here in Acts, and that's what we see in the in the uh early church documents that we have in terms of church order and what deacons do."],[1609.47,"Um so I think the the current Orthodox version of a deacon is closer to what we see here in scripture than the sort of board of deacons that you see, or a lot of Baptist churches will have a board of deacons that again is taking care of the treasure, taking care of the monetary, you know, but it's very focused on that church, is it safe to say that well, for instance, um in Oklahoma there is uh I saw some video series by a subdeacon, title period."],[1642.36,"Um subdeacon Ezra put on a series of seminars for well subdeacon, yeah."],[1648.8,"Yeah, where's where subdeacons come from, and you'll see them once in a while."],[1652.68,"You see them more, in my experience at least."],[1656.16,"Uh you see them more in the US than you do in a l in a lot of places in the old country."],[1661.36,"Um there are subdeacons there."],[1663.2,"They're in Arabic, my understanding, and you can correct me."],[1668.05,"Is that they're both called deacons."],[1671.17,"And there's there's uh Shemas uh and Gil, the deacon of the gospel of the gospel, which is what we just call a deacon because they read the gospel in the liturgy, is one of the things that they do liturgically."],[1684.79,"And then there's is it Shamas al-Nur or something like that?"],[1689.43,"Deacon of the Lhtsig, um, which is what we call a subdeacon."],[1696.31,"Because their their job is primarily to light the candles and care for the lives."],[1698.95,"Okay But what happens is there are a whole in the early church, following after the scriptures."],[1711.23,"We're not going to see this in this level of detail in the New Testament, but there are a whole bunch of bunch of sort of little practical jobs that developed."],[1718.67,"So there are doorkeepers, you know, who are sort of greeters and take care of the doors, and there are people who go and trim all the wicks on the lamps and make sure all the lamps get lit and the can take care of the candles."],[1730.35,"You know, and there are people who do all these jobs."],[1733.31,"And they were all separate titles at one point."],[1736.95,"Um we still sort of had one of them has survived, and that's reader."],[1741.79,"There are still read, you'll still see somebody once in a while wearing a cassock at an Orthodox Church who's a reader."],[1743.95,"Um but there used to be a whole ton of those little jobs"],[1749.59,"And those were sort of all combined into this, into what we call a subdeacon."],[1755.35,"Okay. Um so subdeacons uh will um do all those helpful things."],[1764.71,"So I was a subdeacon for a few years before I became a deacon, before I became a priest."],[1769.59,"Um, and the the one sort of liturgical thing they do is uh part of their job that they inherited from another one of those little offices again, is to help uh the bishop vest before the bishop serves liturgy."],[1782.71,"So if there's a bishop there, and they also will uh carry the candles, the tree carry and decarry the two candles and the three candles for the bishop while he's serving when he's not using them."],[1793.07,"You know, when he's sensing or whatever, they'll carry them."],[1795.95,"Um that's the liturgical side."],[1798.43,"The other side is just they sort of handle all of these sacristan and these other these other duties."],[1808.43,"Yeah, lighting the candles thinking here."],[1810.43,"Yeah, people. Which is important if it doesn't get done."],[1813.31,"You know, they're small things, but you know, but making sure they're all they're all done."],[1818.35,"So that's that's what subdeacons do."],[1820.83,"And um that the the it's usually in English at least referred to as the major offices and the minor offices."],[1830.99,"The major offices are are uh bishop, presbyter or priest and uh deacon and then the minor offices now are really condensed down to just really two that's subdeacon and reader."],[1845.99,"But most of the time Shamaz or the deacon would become a priest at some point."],[1850.94,"Well in the in the finish all his education."],[1853.5,"The lowest of those is a reader."],[1855.66,"Reader or chanter and in the prayer that's prayed when they're tonsured as a reader the word they use the word tonsured instead of ordained because it's a lower office."],[1865.26,"When they're tonsured to that office, the prayer says that they should work and aspire to you know sort of move up."],[1872.94,"You know, um from there uh in the in the clergy."],[1877.82,"Um so yeah, that's that's what a subdeacon is."],[1881.22,"Well in the Episcopal Church we preserved a lot of those offices like verger and uh what was the one we just mentioned uh sacristan."],[1892.26,"Yeah uh and only big churches had them."],[1896.18,"Oh yeah, yeah, and sometimes even the acolytes are broken up and you have a crucifer and you have a you know that's all these really super particular jobs."],[1905.7,"Um they're all sort of rolled together."],[1908.1,"Um well the verger in an English cathedral is really cool."],[1911.78,"He has a he has a sort of wand."],[1915.14,"And he he he leads everybody around and it doesn't allow us to so yeah but um, for example, when our church here didn't have a priest, there was a deacon who would come on occasion and give communion and lead prayers and that kind of thing."],[1936.86,"Uh so yeah, that's that's the deacon's job is to sort of go out, you know, and bring the Eucharist, bring the word, bring the teaching with him, you know, to places sort of to extend the reach of the bishop."],[1950.94,"And so that's what we see here with the these first deacons, is that they're sort of extensions of the apostles' ministry in both mercy and in preaching and teaching."],[1965.34,"Uh one last note here uh about Nicholas the proselyte from Antioch."],[1971.22,"Uh Nicholas, one of the seven deacons, is gonna become one of the first heretics."],[1975.94,"Oh, really? Uh the history of the church."],[1980.02,"Um we're gonna see, he's gonna pop up again, and we'll talk more about it when we get there."],[1985.78,"In the book of Revelation, uh there's gonna be a reference to the Nicolaitans who are followers of Nicholas, and that's this Nicholas, who's going to sort of go off and start his own his own uh schism."],[2003.54,"Um so just like one of the twelve, uh-huh, as we saw in the gospels, one of these seven is going to depart later."],[2012.9,"Okay, so the apostles, you notice they pray and they lay hands on them"],[2018.54,"Uh so um this is important because uh of the idea of ordination."],[2025.98,"Uh we understand ordination, holy orders in the Orthodox Church to be a sacrament."],[2031.34,"Um that is not the case in, for example, Protestant churches."],[2036.54,"Um they do ordinations, obviously, and there are even ceremonies attending ordinations, but uh there's a direct argument that it is not a sacrament."],[2046.06,"Um and uh this this this upsets a bit."],[2051.18,"I have I've had an ongoing conversation with a good friend of mine who may actually listen to this when it's eventually posted online."],[2058.26,"Who it it has bothered him for some time, and he'll bring it up to me that the Orthodox Church does not consider his ordination valid."],[2066.98,"And I always respond by saying, well, valid is a word that's used to describe sacraments."],[2071.7,"And if you don't think your ordination is a sacrament, why should I think your ordination is a sacrament?"],[2079.46,"And so this can you please expand on I'm again I'm coming from the Protestant."],[2089.54,"W whathat does it mean?"],[2090.82,"Okay. Um so uh at a basic level, I mean the the definition of sacrament is for Latin words that means holy things, but that's again not very super. helpful because there's lots of holy things"],[2105.66,"But when we talk about sacrament, the the word that's used more commonly in the Orthodox Church is the mysteries, the holy mysteries."],[2112.62,"Um the idea is for example, I mean the the two basic ones that Protestants pretty much all agree right with us on, if they believe in sacraments at all, is uh baptism and the Eucharist."],[2126.75,"And the word term that's usually used in the West is their means of grace, the means by which we receive God's grace in our lives."],[2135.39,"Okay. Um and output invisible signs of visible and something."],[2142.67,"Yeah, now you're being a Calvinist."],[2144.27,"Yeah. That is, you know, I had to memorize it."],[2146.59,"So I memorized some similar things at one point"],[2146.79,"Yeah. But um, but yeah, so that's that's sort of the the root."],[2156.87,"But uh the core of the idea of the Orthodox Church is the way we understand grace is that it's God's energies."],[2165.51,"And that's not energy like woo, you know, but that's energy is in and ergos, God working in the world."],[2174.07,"Okay. God is at work in the world, he's changing things, he's transforming things, he's doing things."],[2179.05,"And so the mysteries of the church are uh these ordinances through which God does that, through which God changes us, transforms us, shapes us."],[2190.11,"Makes us new."],[2194.51,"And so obviously, this is why pretty much, pretty much all Christians of any variety see baptism as a sacrament."],[2206.88,"Interestingly, even some Protestant groups that really fight and oppose the idea of baptismal regeneration."],[2213.44,"They don't want to say that you're really born again in baptism."],[2218.08,"But they do want to say that baptism is a sacrament, meaning God's grace is working in you through it."],[2224.56,"So, you know, you could work out the contradictions there."],[2227.52,"Just like those who want to say this isn't really the body and blood of Christ, still want to say it's a sacrament."],[2233.44,"It's a means of grace."],[2234.41,"And so there's ways that God is changing you."],[2238.05,"And the the Protestant reformers, um, probably the classic example of this is the first edition."],[2244.21,"There were four editions of Calvin's Institutes of the Christian Religion."],[2247.89,"And the first edition has this great, uh, these great two sections."],[2251.81,"One is entitled, you know, the two sacraments, and the other one is called the five sacraments, falsely so-called."],[2260.13,"So where he goes into why the other five aren't."],[2263.41,"And uh Calvin and then the Reformation and Protestants following him pushed back against the Roman Catholic view of several of these sacraments."],[2276.05,"Uh so for example, uh the Roman Catholic view. of marriage, they pushed back on it and said marriage is not a sacrament"],[2283.85,"Again, there's a practical question there of then why do you do it in a church if it's not a sacrament?"],[2289.61,"But we'll talk about that more later in a passage about marriage."],[2292.41,"To stick to ordination here."],[2294.97,"The Roman Catholic view of ordination is really problematic from an Orthodox perspective, let alone a Protestant perspective."],[2303.85,"They believe that because they have a different view of grace, we believe that Grace is God working directly in his creation."],[2313.13,"And they, in classical Roman Catholic theology, contemporary Roman Catholic theology, you can't say anything about."],[2320.01,"That's true because it's all over the place."],[2322.89,"But classical, classic.ical Roman Cathol theology, grace is a substance"],[2330.69,"It's a stuff."],[2333.09,"And so in classical Roman Catholic theology, this grace substance is deposited in different things."],[2340.69,"It's quantifiable. And so their understanding of the sacraments is that you receive a certain quantity of it through each sacrament."],[2353.87,"And all kinds of things."],[2356.03,"And particularly with ordination, they believe that there is an indelible character placed upon a man who is made a priest, such that that person now functions as an altar Christus, as another Christ."],[2371.27,"Whoa. In fact, there was a recent statement from the head of the Congress on the cardinal, who's the head of the Congress and Liturgics, and so opposing the married priesthood, in which he he went further."],[2385.27,"He said that a priest is not only an altar Christus, but he is uh ipsy Christus, he's Christ himself, following ordination."],[2397.59,"So, and so for example, in uh in the rite of the traditional rite of confession in the Roman Catholic Church, the priest begins the absolution by saying, I absolve you."],[2412.95,"Because the priest is given the power to absolve sins by his ordination."],[2418.99,"And the tradition again, the traditional Roman Catholic teaching is that the priest has the power to call Christ down from heaven and place him on the altar."],[2429.79,"And that by saying the words of institution, because he has the power to do it, he makes the host into the body, blood, soul, and divinity of Christ."],[2439.15,"He has the power to do that."],[2441.95,"And because that's an indelible character which is stamped on the priest in traditional Roman Catholic theology, even if he apostatizes, even if he leaves the church, even if he's excommunicated like Martin Luther, he continues to to cele celebrbrate.ate, to be able to, to have that power, and be able a valid Eucharist for the rest of his life"],[2466.52,"This explains a lot."],[2468.25,"So clearly there are problems with that from an Orthodox perspective, but also obviously the reformers had a problem with that."],[2476.33,"And so they pushed back on that, and rather than just sort of correcting it, they argued this isn't a sacrament at all."],[2486.86,"Nothing is actually done to this person."],[2491.83,"This person who's made a presbyter or a deacon, we're just setting them aside for that job."],[2497.59,"And there's no grace involved."],[2499.37,"And if the time comes that we remove them from that, need to remove them from that job, we remove them from that job."],[2501.77,"They're just holding that job"],[2505.73,"But this is why in a lot of Protestant churches, I mean, especially elders and deacons will rotate on and off."],[2512.27,"I mean, you're you're not one for life because you were one."],[2515.95,"Now, some you are, but some Protestant churches, you just it's just an elect, literally an elected office."],[2521.63,"Like the parish council."],[2523.36,"Um, and so so that is, you know, going to the other extreme."],[2530.88,"Okay. Going to the other extreme."],[2532.96,"Um so we right, and so we as Orthodox Christians understand that God is working, in the person."],[2543.15,"Who is made a priest. or a deacon"],[2546.76,"Or consecrated as a bishop."],[2549.02,"That God is working, that person, God is giving them the grace and working in them and through them to transform them, to prepare them, to make them able to perform the task, tasks that they're going to perform."],[2560.99,"But just like all grace, uh, human beings can, as Hebrews says, trample upon it."],[2567.69,"We could decide to apostasize, we can turn from that grace, we can reject it."],[2572.26,"And that doesn't sort of stay with us."],[2574.68,"There's no power imparted to me as a presbyter."],[2578.84,"In fact, the first words I say when I give absolution after confession is, I have no power on earth to forgive sins but God alone."],[2587.2,"I don't think you can have a more direct contrast between the traditional Roman Catholic Rite and ours."],[2596.8,"So that's sort of the issue there."],[2602.0,"Now, why I bring that up here is what we see is done with these men."],[2608.02,"That there are qualifications for this position."],[2610.9,"This is a position that they're going to hold for the rest of their lives."],[2615.04,"Lord willing. Now, as we already said, Nicholas doesn't."],[2619.04,"He apostasizes. But they're set apart for this, and the apostles don't just say, okay, good, you got seven guys who are up to the job, go to it."],[2628.18,"But they come and they lay hands on them, they pray over them."],[2631.47,"There is an ordination ritual here in the book of Acts."],[2638.75,"You know, maybe a year, maybe a little more than a year after Christ's resurrection."],[2645.23,"This isn't ordination, isn't this idea that develops later."],[2650.41,"A hundred years later when the church becomes an institution and blah blah blah."],[2654.27,"This is something that's happening right from the beginning."],[2656.51,"Is there's this idea that these men are being given this sacred task and this office, and that you know, God is going to provide grace for them."],[2666.67,"And and part of the evidence of that is exactly what they do."],[2671.05,"It's not just that they pray for them and have some sort of commissioning service."],[2672.89,"of And we're going to see later in the book Acts is going to be to receive the Holy Spirit."],[2674.13,"They lay hands on them."],[2675.01,"Where have we seen them lay hands on people before?"],[2684.61,"So this has this idea of imparting grace and empowerment to do what it is that they are now being called to do."],[2693.99,"And so that same sacramental understanding is going to be carried over, and we'll talk about this more, like I said, when we get to marriage, for example."],[2701.11,"That in marriage, what's happening in the in the mystery of marriage in the church is that God is making two people one flesh."],[2708.56,"I, as a priest, don't have the power to do that."],[2711.66,"God is doing that in those people's lives."],[2713.42,"'s lives He working in their through grace to do that."],[2718.58,"They're capable of trampling on that grace."],[2721.96,"Right. And ignoring it, making it of no avail, and you know, whatever."],[2727.4,"But God is there present doing this and empowering this in the sacrament of marriage."],[2736.44,"Now, you referred to the seven sacraments of the Roman Catholic Church, which were not enumerated until the 13th or fourteenth century."],[2748.62,"Um and so I wanted to ask, do we have a specific seven sacraments?"],[2755.62,"I know I also had the idea, this was in my episcopal phase, that some things were sacramentals."],[2764.56,"That comes out of the same Roman Catholic idea."],[2767.04,"Uh-huh. They s made this distinction between sac the seven sacraments, and then there are sacramentals, all these which are sort of all the other churchy things that you do."],[2777.28,"And those are considered to be subsidiary to sort of the big the big seven."],[2781.76,"Yeah. But we are our mysteries."],[2784.08,"What all of those distinctions are pretty much absent in the Orthodox Church."],[2788.56,"Now you will find particularly in modern Orthodox documents, they will sometimes talk about seven sacraments or seven mysteries."],[2798.16,"And then they roughly parallel things we do in the Orthodox Church with the seven Roman Catholic sacraments."],[2804.28,"Some of those are pretty close, like baptism."],[2807.29,"The Eucharist, where we're doing something clearly similar."],[2810.57,"Some of them, like the difference between the way we do unction, anointing with oil, and last rites."],[2816.73,"There's more difference."],[2818.25,"Yeah, there's more difference that's evolved."],[2821.21,"I personally think that's unhelpful."],[2823.45,"If you really go to Orthodox sources that aren't in dialogue with Roman Catholicism, that aren't in dialogue with the West, you will find some different numbers. you'll find 10 and 12"],[2835.45,"You'll find and twelve. things."],[2839.53,"More commonly, you just they won't be numbered."],[2843.69,"And so, you know, things like the consecration of the Emperor, the tonsuring of a monk to enter the monastic life, uh, the great blessing of water on Theophany, you know, where we bless holy water, um, are all treated as mysteries of the church, you know, um, in the same sort of way, for just as some examples."],[2871.33,"Um, so yeah, it's it's harder to draw that line."],[2875.41,"We do have things that at least these roughly parallel the seven sacraments of the Roman Catholic Church."],[2884.53,"So that's not totally invalid to set those things in parallel and explain the similarities and differences."],[2890.13,"I'm not saying that, but I tried to say that the Orthodox Church, in and of herself, part of that understands there to be seven mysteries that are different than everything else we do, and that it's those seven, I think you'd be hard pressed to make that argument just from the Orthodox ethos by itself."],[2913.45,"Father Alexander Sprieman famously uh argued that at one level everything is a sacrament."],[2921.29,"At one level, the entire creation is a sacrament because we receive uh God as working through all of it."],[2927.21,"You know, and we receive grace through all of it."],[2932.25,"So, you know, there's that too."],[2934.25,"But that if you take that too too far to an extreme, that becomes unhelpful too, because then it's like, well pantheism."],[2942.61,"Yeah, well, not just that, but then it's like, well, okay, yeah, but there's a difference between receiving the Eucharist and going on a hike in the mountains."],[2952.21,"I mean, we're too big, all things holy make nothing holy."],[2956.21,"Yeah. Uh-huh. So that if taken too far, but he I mean he has a point."],[2960.21,"Uh-huh. And that God is working through the whole created order."],[2963.47,"And through ev he's working our lives through every event of our lives."],[2966.75,"He was trying to sort of get over or get past this idea where we we, as modern secular people, tend to separate our lives into, okay, well, here's my secular life that's very mundane and black and white and gray and drab, and then then I go to church for this beautiful. feast and this beautiful liturgy, you know, and there's sort of the spiritual part and the secular"],[2987.35,"And he's trying to say no, really, God's trying to speak to you over in the secular part too."],[2993.67,"The only reason it's black and white and gray is because you're not listening."],[2997.75,"You know. That there's more continuity there than you're allowing."],[3002.79,"But you, like I said, you could take his his point too far in the other direction."],[3008.71,"Um, but so yeah, we see here the ordinate the idea of ordination, the basic concept of ordination is already here at the very beginning."],[3018.95,"R rightight? In the way this is handled with the with the ordination of these seven deacons."],[3025.63,"And we'll talk about that more as we get later in Acts as we start to see presbyters pop up, too."],[3028.47,"But uh here it's here it's the first seven deacons"],[3036.19,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3043.55,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3048.91,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.8,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: where we are in Acts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Acts 6:1</b>; first Acts study online has his long intro</li><li class=\"d0\">Still the <b>Jerusalem phase</b> of the plan: witnesses in <b>Jerusalem → Judea → Samaria → ends of the earth</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pentecost began growth: <b>120 in the room → thousands</b></li><li class=\"d1\">visitors for the feasts hear, then <b>go back home</b> carrying what they heard - we'll meet some later</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Barnabas</b> glimpsed: from <b>Cyprus</b>, sold property, gave the money</li><li class=\"d0\">Preaching in the temple (as Christ did the week before the crucifixion); leaders oppose apostles as they opposed him</li><li class=\"d1\">arrested <b>twice</b>: first a stern warning; second all the <b>rabbinical \"big guns\"</b> incl. <b>Gamaliel</b></li></ul>"},{"t":175.72,"lv":3,"h":"Gamaliel's counsel, recapped","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Gamaliel counseled caution: <b>would-be messiahs</b> killed → movements <b>petered out</b></li><li class=\"d1\">still close to the crucifixion - if not from God, \"death throes of the movement\"</li><li class=\"d1\">if it doesn't peter out, good evidence it's from God</li><li class=\"d0\">→ \"whether true or not, best thing is <b>nothing</b>\"; they had the apostles <b>beaten</b>, then nothing</li><li class=\"d0\">Apostles <b>rejoiced</b> to suffer, went out <b>more vigorously</b> in Jerusalem</li></ul>"},{"t":263.32,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 6:1 - Complaint of the Hellenists","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"When the number of disciples was multiplying, a complaint arose against the Hebrews by the Hellenists because their widows were neglected in the daily distribution\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Community growing; members <b>sold what they had</b>, shared goods and food</li><li class=\"d1\">bigger community → <b>logistical problems</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Widows and orphans</b> throughout Scripture = <b>endangered class</b></li><li class=\"d1\">only adult men could <b>testify in court</b> or get employment - \"no one was going to hire a woman to be a farmhand\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God in the <b>Torah</b>: <b>protector of widows and orphans</b>, stands up for their cause, opposes anyone who harms them</li><li class=\"d0\">Money thrown into a common pot → food bought and distributed <b>daily</b> → widows and orphans must get what they need</li></ul>"},{"t":385.59,"lv":3,"h":"Hebrews vs Hellenists: language","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hellene</b> strictly = Greek; but here <b>two kinds of Jews</b>, not Jews and Greeks</li><li class=\"d0\">Terms describe the <b>language they speak</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Palestine's linguistic situation \"very complicated\"</li><li class=\"d1\">anyone educated, or from <b>Galilee</b> (\"Galilee of the Gentiles\" - Greek-speaking and Roman cities), doing commerce - <b>Greek</b> (fishermen selling in a market)</li><li class=\"d1\">common people, peasants: <b>Aramaic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jerusalem and around: a <b>local dialect of Hebrew</b> - Hebrew is basically a dialect of Aramaic; not identical to OT Hebrew, which only the very educated (rabbis, Pharisees, scribes) knew</li><li class=\"d0\">So <b>Hebrews</b> = native population of Jerusalem, lived there all along</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hellenists</b> = diaspora Jews, primarily Greek-speaking - even Galileans who've come and joined</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Barnabas</b> from Cyprus = perfect example</li></ul>"},{"t":572.29,"lv":3,"h":"Insiders and outsiders","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Core: <b>locals</b> vs <b>newcomers from outside</b> who joined</li><li class=\"d0\">Outsiders' complaint: <b>their widows</b> get less attention than the well-known locals</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: would Hellenist widows have made the pilgrimage? - expensive?</li><li class=\"d1\">could be <b>sponsored by relatives</b>, or just <b>walked</b>, stayed with a relative</li><li class=\"d1\">camp at stations on the way to Jerusalem - <b>long journey</b>, days or a week - not impossible</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Insiders/outsiders</b> thing: locals know each other and care for each other</li><li class=\"d1\">not paying as much attention to the <b>new converts</b></li></ul>"},{"t":699.36,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 6:2 - \"Serve tables\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>The Twelve</b> gather <b>the multitude of the disciples</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>disciple</b> = Greek for <b>learner</b> → learning from the apostles</li><li class=\"d1\">ultimately disciples of <b>Christ</b> through them - continuing to teach what Christ taught (Luke, start of Acts)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"It is not desirable that we should leave the Word of God and serve tables\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Word of God</b> ≠ Bible: \"no Bible at this point\"</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>preaching</b>; like the prophets, Word of God proclaimed, <b>not their own opinion</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT books exist only as <b>individual scrolls</b>; synagogues needed vast donations to buy a Torah scroll</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter didn't carry one to study at night</li><li class=\"d0\">Task from Jesus: <b>\"you will be my witnesses\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we hear your complaints, but we can't stop preaching to manage the finer points of food distribution\" - important, but <b>own tasks</b></li></ul>"},{"t":877.63,"lv":3,"h":"Parallel: Jethro and Moses","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Another Luke parallel to the OT: <b>Jethro</b> visits <b>Moses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Moses sits all day on disputes - \"so-and-so killed my goat\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jethro: \"you can't do this all day every day\" → appoint others → <b>70 elders</b> over the tribes</li><li class=\"d0\">Same here: resolving these things matters, but a <b>particular calling</b> on the apostles</li><li class=\"d0\">Solution isn't quit complaining → <b>bring in people to oversee</b>, as Moses brought in elders, so it's done in the <b>correct way</b></li></ul>"},{"t":961.56,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 6:3-4 - Seven men","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Seek out from among you seven men of good reputation, full of the Holy Spirit and wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"We will give ourselves continually to prayer and to the ministry of the word\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">calling: <b>prayer and preaching Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">You pick seven</li></ul>"},{"t":990.02,"lv":3,"h":"Why seven: number of wholeness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Seven in Scripture = <b>completion, wholeness</b>; seven days of creation - the whole world</li><li class=\"d0\">Derives from <b>seven planets</b> (ancient view; \"sounds crazy in our modern world\")</li><li class=\"d1\">no telescopes or observatories; stars move in <b>fixed patterns</b> by season</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>planet</i> = Greek <b>wanderer</b>: <b>seven objects</b> not following the star patterns - sun and moon among them</li><li class=\"d1\">reasoning: brighter and bigger = closer → sun, moon, Venus</li><li class=\"d0\">Seven wanderers = the <b>whole cosmos</b> → seven = whole, complete</li></ul>"},{"t":1103.01,"lv":2,"h":"Qualifications of the seven","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Good reputation, full of the Holy Spirit and wisdom</b> - not \"the new guy who showed up last week\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Tradition: they came from the <b>70</b> (Jesus' seventy disciples)</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter, James, John → the Twelve → the 70</li><li class=\"d1\">had been <b>with Jesus</b>, \"been with us a long time\", know what's going on</li><li class=\"d1\">not recent converts, not <b>super young</b> and unsteady</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Whole multitude</b> pleased - all agree</li></ul>"},{"t":1166.75,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 6:5-6 - The seven chosen","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Stephen</b> (full of faith and the Holy Spirit), <b>Philip, Prochorus, Nicanor, Timon, Parmenas, Nicolas</b>, proselyte from Antioch</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Proselyte</b> ≠ convert to Christianity: <b>not ethnically Jewish</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Greek name <b>Nicolaus</b>; joined the Jewish community at Antioch; now accepts Jesus as Messiah</li><li class=\"d0\">Set before the apostles; <b>prayed and laid hands on them</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1228.59,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: \"deacon\" and \"presbyter\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: word \"deacon\" not yet used? - he'll discuss it; Q's background Presbyterian: <b>elders and deacons</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>presbyter</i> = elder; <b>presbyopia</b> = \"old eyes\"; originally Greek \"<b>a person with a beard</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Protestant view: deacons = <b>practical matters, treasury, money</b> (like a parish council); elders = spiritual life</li><li class=\"d1\">Orthodox deacons: <b>liturgical</b> work</li><li class=\"d0\">Words alone don't tell: <i>presbyter</i> = older man, not all older men; <i>diakonos</i> = <b>servant</b>, household servant</li><li class=\"d0\">In Acts: Stephen, Philip etc. <b>preach, evangelize, lead prayers</b> - extra duties beyond the food</li><li class=\"d1\">early sources: <b>Didache</b> (early 2nd c.), <b>Apostolic Constitutions</b> (3rd-4th c.)</li><li class=\"d1\">more later in Paul's <b>pastoral epistles</b> (1-2 Timothy, Titus): presbyters, deacons, qualifications</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>women deacons</b> discussion later - <b>deaconesses</b> before Acts ends; here all men</li></ul>"},{"t":1468.88,"lv":3,"h":"What deacons did in the early Church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Dual role</b>: liturgical, preaching and teaching role, <b>subsidiary to the bishop</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Charged with <b>visiting the sick</b>, bringing the <b>Eucharist</b> to shut-ins, elderly, widows</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"finances per se\" - churches then not small <b>nonprofit corporations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>ministry of mercy</b>; extension of the <b>bishop's ministry</b> beyond the church's walls</li><li class=\"d1\">so the bishop (like the apostles) can devote himself to <b>prayer, preaching, teaching</b></li><li class=\"d0\">For centuries <b>seven deacons per city</b> from this passage (e.g. bishop of Antioch)</li><li class=\"d0\">Today's Orthodox deacon closer to Scripture than a Baptist <b>board of deacons</b> (treasury, that church only)</li><li class=\"d1\">illustration: when their parish had no priest, a deacon came, gave communion, led prayers</li></ul>"},{"t":1609.47,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: subdeacons and minor offices","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: subdeacons - seminars online by a subdeacon</li><li class=\"d0\">More in the <b>US</b> than \"old country\"; Arabic: <b>Shamas al-Injil</b> (deacon of the Gospel) vs <b>deacon of the light</b> = subdeacon</li><li class=\"d1\">job: <b>light candles</b>, care for the lamps</li><li class=\"d0\">Early church: lots of <b>little practical jobs</b> - doorkeepers/greeters, wick-trimmers - separate titles</li><li class=\"d1\">one survives: <b>reader</b> (cassock); rest combined into <b>subdeacon</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Subdeacon: helps the <b>bishop vest</b>, carries the <b>two and three candles</b> when he isn't using them; sacristan duties</li><li class=\"d1\">he was one a few years before deacon, then priest</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Major offices</b>: bishop, presbyter, deacon; <b>minor</b>: subdeacon, reader</li><li class=\"d1\">reader/chanter <b>tonsured</b> not ordained; prayer says aspire to move up</li><li class=\"d0\">Episcopal: <b>verger</b> (wand), sacristan, acolytes, crucifer - \"really super particular jobs\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Deacon: go out, bring the <b>Eucharist, the word, the teaching</b> - extend the reach of the bishop</li><li class=\"d0\">First deacons = extensions of the apostles' ministry in <b>mercy and preaching</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1965.34,"lv":2,"h":"Nicholas, the Nicolaitans","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Nicholas</b> becomes one of the <b>first heretics</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Revelation</b>: <b>Nicolaitans</b>, followers of this Nicholas, who starts his own <b>schism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like one of the Twelve (Judas), one of the seven departs</li></ul>"},{"t":2012.9,"lv":2,"h":"Ordination as a sacrament","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Apostles <b>pray and lay hands</b> - the idea of <b>ordination</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox: <b>holy orders = a sacrament</b>; Protestants: obviously ordain, but argue it's <b>not</b></li><li class=\"d1\">friend's complaint: Orthodox don't consider his ordination <b>valid</b> → \"<b>valid</b> describes sacraments; if you don't think yours is one, why should I?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: what does sacrament mean?</li><li class=\"d1\">Latin for \"holy things\" - not helpful; Orthodox: <b>mysteries</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Protestants agree on <b>baptism and Eucharist</b>; West: <b>means of grace</b> (\"outward visible sign of inward grace\" - Calvinist memorization)</li><li class=\"d1\">Orthodox: grace = <b>God's energies</b> (<i>energeia</i>, God working in the world) - mysteries = ordinances through which God <b>changes, transforms, makes us new</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2194.51,"lv":3,"h":"Sacraments among Protestants and Rome","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Nearly all Christians see <b>baptism</b> as a sacrament</li><li class=\"d1\">even groups against <b>baptismal regeneration</b> say God's grace works through it; same with Eucharist not \"really the body and blood\" but a means of grace</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Calvin's Institutes</b>, first of four editions: \"the two sacraments\" and \"<b>the five sacraments, falsely so-called</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">pushed back on Rome: e.g. <b>marriage</b> not a sacrament - \"then why do it in a church?\" (later)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Roman Catholic</b> view of ordination problematic even for Protestants</li><li class=\"d1\">classical: grace is a <b>substance</b>, \"a stuff\" deposited, quantifiable per sacrament</li><li class=\"d1\">ordination: <b>indelible character</b> → <i>alter Christus</i>; recent cardinal statement: <i>ipse Christus</i>, Christ himself</li><li class=\"d1\">confession: \"<b>I absolve you</b>\"; priest has power to <b>call Christ down</b>; words of institution make the host body, blood, soul and divinity</li><li class=\"d1\">even if he apostatizes or is excommunicated (<b>Martin Luther</b>), keeps the power for a valid Eucharist for life</li></ul>"},{"t":2468.25,"lv":2,"h":"Protestant reaction vs Orthodox view","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reformers: instead of correcting, said <b>not a sacrament at all</b>; nothing done to the person</li><li class=\"d1\">just <b>set aside for a job</b>, removable; elders and deacons <b>rotate</b>, elected like a parish council - \"the other extreme\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox: God works <b>in the person</b> made priest, deacon, bishop - gives grace to prepare and enable</li><li class=\"d1\">like all grace can be <b>trampled</b> (Hebrews), rejected, apostasy; no power stays imparted</li><li class=\"d1\">his words at absolution: <b>\"I have no power on earth to forgive sins but God alone\"</b> - direct contrast with the Roman rite</li></ul>"},{"t":2602.0,"lv":3,"h":"Ordination in Acts from the start","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Qualifications, set apart <b>for life</b> - Nicholas apostasizes</li><li class=\"d0\">Apostles <b>lay hands and pray</b> - ordination ritual in Acts, about a year after the resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">not a later idea when \"the church becomes an institution\"</li><li class=\"d1\">later in Acts, laying on hands to <b>receive the Holy Spirit</b> → imparting grace and empowerment</li><li class=\"d0\">Same sacramental view for <b>marriage</b>: God makes two <b>one flesh</b>; priest has no power to do that</li><li class=\"d1\">couple can trample the grace; God present empowering</li></ul>"},{"t":2736.44,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: how many sacraments?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: seven sacraments not enumerated until the <b>13th-14th c.</b>; <b>sacramentals</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Distinction comes from Rome: seven + sacramentals as subsidiary - <b>absent</b> in Orthodoxy</li><li class=\"d1\">modern Orthodox documents speak of <b>seven mysteries</b>, paralleling Roman ones; baptism, Eucharist close; <b>unction</b> vs last rites more different - he finds it unhelpful</li><li class=\"d1\">sources not in dialogue with the West: <b>10, 12</b>, or <b>not numbered</b></li><li class=\"d1\">consecration of an emperor, <b>tonsuring a monk</b>, <b>great blessing of water at Theophany</b> = all mysteries</li><li class=\"d0\">Can't argue from Orthodox ethos alone that the seven are different from everything else</li></ul>"},{"t":2913.45,"lv":3,"h":"Schmemann: everything a sacrament","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Fr Alexander Schmemann</b>: at one level <b>creation is a sacrament</b> - grace through all of it</li><li class=\"d1\">too far → pantheism; \"difference between the Eucharist and a hike in the mountains\"; \"all things holy make nothing holy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">His point: God works through <b>every event of our lives</b></li><li class=\"d1\">against secular people splitting a <b>drab, mundane</b> life from beautiful church</li><li class=\"d1\">God speaks in the secular part too - \"only reason it's black and white and gray is you're not listening\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Back to Acts: <b>ordination</b> concept already here at the very start with the <b>first seven deacons</b>; presbyters later</li></ul>"}]});
